  "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-3\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=3\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3\\text{i})(x+3\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 3\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $-5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+9) & =0 \\\\ -5{x}^{2}-45 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
